              "text": "The iconic episode where Del Boy falls through the bar is titled **\"Yuppy Love\"**. It is the first episode of **Series 6** and originally aired on **January 8, 1989**. \n\nIt is widely considered one of the greatest and most famous comedy moments in British television history.\n\n### The Scene\nDel Boy (David Jason) has adopted a new \"yuppie\" persona (complete with red braces, a briefcase, and a filofax) after being inspired by the movie *Wall Street*. He and Trigger (Roger Lloyd-Pack) visit a trendy wine bar to try and impress two women they spot across the room.\n\nLeaning on the bar, Del famously tells Trigger:\n> *\"Play it nice and cool, son, nice and cool, you know what I mean?\"*\n\nAs Del turns to gesture to the girls, the bartender lifts the bar flap to come out from behind the counter. Still talking, Del confidently leans back to rest his elbow on what he thinks is the solid bar\u2014and falls straight through the gap onto the floor. \n\nThe comedic brilliance is heightened by Trigger\u2019s reaction: completely oblivious to the fall, he does a slow double-take, looking around the room in absolute bewilderment, wondering where Del has suddenly vanished to. After a moment, Del pops back up, brushes himself off, and tells Trigger, *\"I think we're on a winner here... come on, you're cramping my style,\"* before they walk away.\n\n---\n\n### Fascinating Behind-the-Scenes Facts\n* **It was a last-minute addition:** The scene was not originally in the script. The episode was running about five minutes too short, so writer John Sullivan wrote the bar scene at the last minute to pad the runtime.\n* **Inspired by real life:** John Sullivan got the idea after visiting a pub and watching a man nearly fall through a bar hatch in a similar manner. He realized if the person actually fell, it would be hilarious.\n* **Trigger wasn\u2019t supposed to be there:** A rehearsal script discovered in 2023 revealed that Trigger wasn't originally written into the scene. The actor, Roger Lloyd-Pack, just happened to show up on set that day. Sullivan quickly wrote him in, creating the legendary \"confused reaction\" that made the scene so perfect. \n* **The physical stunt:** To make the fall look completely natural, David Jason had to fall perfectly straight (without flinching or putting his hands out to break the fall) onto a hidden mattress behind the bar. Because of the setup, they only had one or two chances to get it right.",
